plan StoriesAndModels
title   Deterministic Stories, fixtures, recipes, and pure models
status  Proposed; pending approval
goal
    Let consumers describe deterministic application scenarios, retain truthful evidence, and
    explore pure machine paths before proving them on the production Runtime. This proposed
    capability owns its API, types, behavior, exclusions, and checks within this Plan.

outcomes
    O1  Closed inert Story builders
        - Expose non-callable namespace methods story.app(runtimeSetup, options?),
        story.machine(machine, options?), and story.actor(machine, options?). Exact
        input/context/fixture options, persistent branching, and literal checkpoint typing
        follow below.

        - Helper aliases are specification notation and need not be exported. App Stories require
        typed RuntimeSetup, never bare App or live Runtime. Machine Stories use a private
        one-machine AppPlan and one fresh implicit actor on the production engine. Options are
        closed.

        - Metadata is title?, description?, tags?; maxTurns defaults to 100. Metadata does not
        affect identity. Exact non-void input is required; void input is forbidden. Focused
        Stories require exact selected context when present and forbid it when absent.

        - Builders contain no live handles, Runtime, loops, assertions, predicates, branches,
        arbitrary callbacks, raw memory/state/snapshot overrides, boot unions, refs, or extra
        actors. Every command returns a new plan and leaves its prefix unchanged.

        - Derived runs replay the prefix independently in fresh isolated Runtimes. This is static
        branching with no live fork. Checkpoint names accumulate as exact literal/known-union
        keys.

        - Duplicate names, widened strings, and unknown literal property access reject; other
        builders preserve known names. @V1 proves construction, type negatives, prefix
        preservation, and run isolation.

        ```ts
        type InputOptions<M> = InputOf<M> extends void
          ? { input?: never }
          : { input: InputOf<M> };
        ```

        ```ts
        type SelectedContextOptions<M> = keyof SelectedContextOf<M> extends never
          ? { context?: never }
          : { context: SelectedContextOf<M> };
        ```

        ```ts
        type ContextBindingOptions<M> = keyof SelectedContextOf<M> extends never
          ? { contextBindings?: never }
          : { contextBindings: StoryContextBindingsOf<M> };
        ```

        ```ts
        type StoryContextBindingsOf<M> = {
          [K in keyof SelectedContextOf<M>]:
            | ActorRef<ProviderMachineAt<M, K>>
            | StoryActorRecipe<ProviderMachineAt<M, K>>;
        };
        ```

        ```ts
        type FixtureOptions<Owner> = RequirementsOf<Owner> extends never
          ? { fixtures?: FixtureDefinition[] }
          : { fixtures: FixtureTupleClosing<RequirementsOf<Owner>> };
        ```

        ```ts
        type AppStoryOptions<App> = FixtureOptions<App> & {
          maxTurns?: number;
          title?: string;
          description?: string;
          tags?: string[];
        };
        ```

        ```ts
        type MachineStoryOptions<M> = InputOptions<M> &
          SelectedContextOptions<M> &
          FixtureOptions<M> & {
            maxTurns?: number;
            title?: string;
            description?: string;
            tags?: string[];
          };
        ```

        ```ts
        type ActorRecipeOptions<M> = InputOptions<M> & ContextBindingOptions<M>;
        ```

        - ProviderMachineAt, StoryActorRecipe, FixtureTupleClosing, CheckpointEvidence,
        EndEvidence, and implementation-layer notation need not be exported.

        - Implementation<Output, ImplementationError> is a complete service graph; combined
        outputs satisfy Output and acquisition may fail with its exact ImplementationError.
        Seeds never close a missing service requirement. Fixture, Story and model requirements
        follow @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O2. Their inferred RequirementsOf unions survive complete
        provider installation. @V1, @V2 and @V5 include those exact compile cases.

        ```ts
        type NewCheckpointName<Names extends string, Name extends string> = string extends Name
          ? never
          : Extract<Name, Names> extends never
            ? Name
            : never;

        interface StoryPlan<Names extends string = never> {
          checkpoint<const Name extends string>(
            name: NewCheckpointName<Names, Name>,
          ): StoryPlan<Names | Name>;
          run(options?: { signal?: AbortSignal }): Promise<StoryRun<Names>>;
        }

        type StoryRun<Names extends string> = {
          checkpoints: { [Name in Names]: CheckpointEvidence };
          end: EndEvidence;
        };
        ```

        - Every other command returns a new plan while preserving Names. No widened string erases
        the known-name map. The fragment uses specification-only evidence aliases; exact
        app/machine evidence is inferred.

        - Supporting syntax: [persistent 
        branches](references/README.md).
        Exact option and checkpoint type laws remain above; @V1 owns their proofs.

        - Commands accept validated EventOf envelopes, not constructor Result values.
        Persistent prefixes and inferred keys are required independently of the illustration. 
        Private
        JavaScript/CLI lookup checks own keys and rejects unknown or inherited names with
        existing FlowUsageError semantics. It adds no public function or error type.

    O2  Complete fixtures and exact actor recipes
        - fixture({ id, implementation, seeds? }) supplies complete service Implementations and
        optional Runtime-owned resource seeds. The exact closure/recipe laws follow here.
        Missing external requirements reject even when seeds make a lookup unnecessary.

        - Fixture providers override App providers by service identity. Duplicate fixture
        providers reject rather than choosing by order. Providers construct once per Runtime.
        Every run has fresh scoped state unless application ownership explicitly shares it.

        - Recipes contain exact machine, fresh required input, and exact contextBindings only.
        Provider recipe bindings translate to their exact run-local opaque refs. Such refs are
        valid live Story providers; persistence never promotes them into durable providers. The
        same recipe object resolves one actor per run; distinct recipes remain independent.
        Repeated keys may bind one provider.

        - Bindings accept exact compatible admitted ActorRefs or recipes, including stable refs
        and transitive providers. Validate missing/extra/foreign/mismatched/disposed/cyclic
        dependencies before construction.

        - Materialize providers first, retain production createActor leases, and observe only
        lease.actor. Dispose Story leases in reverse dependency order; app-owned shared actors
        remain Runtime-owned.

        - Fixtures preserve authored input, success, typed failure, cancellation, and lifetime by
        replacing complete service functions. They never replace operation kernels, mock pending
        occurrences, install matchers/control registries, or authorize arbitrary cache mutation.

        - @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O2 owns Implementation closure.
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O4 and @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5 own leases and
        lifecycle. @plan.Resources.O1 owns canonical seed identity. @V2 proves fixture and
        recipe boundaries.

    O3  Production execution, explicit time, and cleanup
        - Both Story kinds support process(), advance(duration), advanceTo(epochMilliseconds),
        advanceToNextTimer(), checkpoint(name), and run({ signal? }). App adds
        send(target,event); machine adds send(event) and setContext(context).

        - App targets an exact recipe or app-owned ActorRef, never a machine family. App Stories
        never inject selected context directly.

        - Changed/equal machine setContext uses production turns and a silent initial baseline.
        run(options?: { signal?: AbortSignal }): Promise<StoryRun<Names>> is the sole execution
        boundary.

        - Each App run calls RuntimeSetup.construct() once and owns a fresh production Runtime,
        fixture capabilities, deterministic TestClock, and isolated in-memory Persistence. Do
        not inherit an external Persistence instance.

        - Use production bootstrap, restoration, AppPlan validation, sealing, activation, ensure,
        acknowledged mailbox dispatch, and cleanup. process drains ready
        mailbox/context/reconciliation/same-time deadline/scheduler/finite work until another
        command or future time is required.

        - It does not drain unrelated work, move time, invent results, intercept pending work, or
        hide pending external calls/streams. Unknown finite work reaching maxTurns fails. Clock
        movement never processes implicitly.

        - Cancellation closes admission, stops later commands, preserves partial evidence, and
        waits for non-abortable finalization. Cleanup retains terminal lifecycle evidence and
        drains the accepted prefix.

        - Close sinks/queues; release Story leases in reverse dependency order; dispose Runtime
        owners; finalize fixture/host scopes last. Run every phase deterministically.
        Execution/cancellation failure remains primary; otherwise the first cleanup failure is
        primary.

        - Either rejects one FlowStoryExecutionError envelope with complete Cause, checkpoints,
        optional end, failure boundary, primary/ordered cleanup diagnostics, cancellation, and
        accepted/drained sequence facts. No constructor signature is invented.

        - Cleanup failure may retain captured end but cannot return successful evidence.
        Story transaction evidence follows @plan.Transactions.O4. It records Flow attempts without
        capturing the adapter's private remote identity or journal or acquiring recovery authority.
        @plan.Statecharts.O3 owns turns; @plan.Statecharts.O4 owns timers and refresh fencing.
        @plan.DiagnosticsContract.O2 owns Cause classification. @V3 proves
        command/time/finalization.

    O4  Atomic checkpoint and end evidence
        - A checkpoint captures immediately. run.end follows commands and precedes cleanup; it is
        not actor finality. Capture the exact static Story closure: the focused machine actor,
        or every app recipe plus exact binding/target refs and transitive providers.

        - Exclude unrelated actors. Story selection does not require durability and does not use
        persistence filtering. Use one atomic cut over committed published snapshots, one
        StoreState revision, runtime pendingWork, TestClock now, actor issues, and accepted
        evidence prefix.

        - Apply @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O7 publication/capture and @plan.Persistence.O3
        consistency laws; Story membership remains owned here. Suspended context debt yields
        retryable ConcurrentCapture rather than an incomplete cut.

        - This does not impose graph-wide simultaneous publication; @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O6
        owns causally complete consumer settlement. App evidence reads
        checkpoint.actor(recipe | exact ActorRef).snapshot.
        Machine evidence exposes snapshot directly.
        The app actor key is an exact recipe object or admitted ActorRef in the captured closure.

        - Both expose runtime.now and runtime.pendingWork; run.end has the same evidence forms.
        Lookup uses captured data, performs no live lookup/work/progress/time
        movement/create/restore/dispose, and retains no authority to resume a Runtime.

        - Flow copies envelopes, records, arrays, readers, keys, issues, and snapshot containers;
        application opaque values retain identity. Release read/selection leases after run.end
        is captured. Failed capture never fabricates end. Completed checkpoints survive later
        failure.

        - Private untrusted-name lookup uses own-key membership and existing FlowUsageError
        semantics; inherited/unknown keys reject. No public dynamic getter or
        checkpoint-specific error exists.

        - @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O3 owns accepted evidence order.
        @plan.InspectionAndArtifacts.O4 owns wire projections.
        @V4 proves capture, failure truth, immutable evidence, and exact lookup.

        - Supporting syntax: [evidence reads](references/README.md).
        Checkpoints are evidence, never resumable snapshots.

    O5  Pure model traversal and live paths
        - model(baseStory, { stateKey }) accepts only a fresh command-empty machine Story.

        - Preserve exact machine/input/selected context/fixtures/progress policy and the empty
        command tuple. stateKey receives the exact predicted snapshot and returns
        CanonicalKeyInput covering every guard/redirect-relevant state, memory, and inert
        exact-ref seed fact. Exact snapshot member names remain open at @Q4.

        - No fallback to object identity or state token exists. @plan.Resources.O1 owns
        canonical-key validation. Only getShortestPaths/getSimplePaths are exposed.

        - Their exact options are { events, maxDepth?, limit? }; events belong to each traversal
        call. maxDepth defaults to 20 and is a non-negative safe integer. limit defaults to 100
        and is a positive safe integer. Event cost is one.

        - Shortest paths use stable candidate-order BFS and first path per unseen key. Simple
        paths use candidate-order DFS with no repeated key in a path. Include the zero-step
        path.

        - Stop at either bound; return { paths, truncated, explored }. truncated is true when a
        bound hides an otherwise reachable candidate. Defects/noncanonical keys throw
        synchronously without partial results.

        - Traversal uses pure transition/guard/redirect/memory logic and inert seeds only. It
        performs no Effects, service acquisition, fixture construction, Runtime/actor work,
        operation execution, mutation, restoration, time movement, or hidden candidate
        retention/registries.

        - Each path retains predicted final projection, per-step data/issues, weight, description,
        and traversal metadata. path.story extends the base with exact authored candidate events
        and empty checkpoints.

        - It is an ordinary Story; path.story.run() supplies live proof and
        path.story.process().run() explicitly drains ready work. Compare final and prefix keys
        using the same stateKey. Async outcomes are traversable only as already-authored
        candidate events.

        - Predict no primitive snapshots, synthesize no routes/checkpoints, and create no second
        interpreter. React attachment remains outside the machine timeline. @V5 proves
        deterministic traversal, bounds, purity, and live final/prefix parity.

        - Supporting syntax: [pure model paths](references/README.md).
        @V5 owns the live final/prefix parity checks; examples report no execution.

        - Candidate events are validated envelopes.

        - Traversal returns paths/truncated/explored under @O5. Returned Stories use ordinary
        evidence, errors, checkpoints, and cleanup when run.

    O6  Inert behavior gateway
        - behavior({ app, stories }) requires one supplied App and a non-empty Story record.
        Record keys are external IDs. Reject empty/duplicate IDs, missing/mixed App identity,
        recipes, incompatible App Stories, and machine Stories outside supplied App.M.

        - App Stories use their supplied RuntimeSetup. Machine Stories may use a package-private
        focused AppPlan without creating a second public app identity. Brand and AppPlan/gateway
        internals remain private.

        - Validation/registration is synchronous and acquires no Implementation, Runtime, actor,
        Story execution, fixture, sink, executor, or cleanup. The CLI is the only public
        consumer.

        - @plan.CommandLine.O1 owns trusted loading, containment, exact package identity, and
        process discovery. @V6 proves inert typed gateway behavior.

decisions
    D1  Stories execute on the production Runtime
        - Test hosts provide configuration, clock, complete services, and seeds before
        construction. They own assertions, pass/fail, retries, and naming after run(), including
        rejected evidence. They do not own a second engine, scheduler, store, evidence model, or
        cleanup system.
    D2  Model predictions require live parity
        - Pure exploration does not prove effects, cross-actor orchestration, or host lifecycle.
        Run returned ordinary Stories to establish relevant production behavior.
    D3  Evidence capture has a distinct membership policy
        - Story target closure and durable persistence closure use shared consistency laws but
        retain separate selection. Nonpersistent Story actors remain valid evidence targets.

constraints
    C1  Keep the public surface closed
        - Reject callable story, .with, bare App/live Runtime inputs,
        perform/deliver/receive/simulate/flush/settle/setTime/final/replay, pending-result
        injection, and synthetic suspend/resume.

        - Reject fork/restore/fromCheckpoint, shared live states/handles/Runtimes, public testing
        runtimes, result/path class hierarchies, replay/provide/clock helpers, and recipe use in
        React or ordinary Runtime APIs.

        - FlowStoryExecutionError is the sole named testing runtime class. Specification helper
        aliases in this Plan are not required exports.
    C2  Models do not acquire executable authority
        - No app Story reduction, boot/nonfresh restore, inferred events, filters, source/target
        selectors, custom weights/duplicate policies/serializers, fixture-outcome persistence,
        function-mocking registry, transaction Effects, stream programs, patches, fibers,
        queues, Deferreds, or Scopes appear in model artifacts or traversal.
    C3  Preserve platform, routes, and evidence status
        - @plan.PackageContract.O1 owns temporary testing-route deferral and restoration gates.
        Checks below are PLANNED; no proposed contract runtime, type, or integration receipt is
        claimed.

verification
    V1  Closed constructors, checkpoint types, and isolated persistent branching
        - Establishes @O1. PLANNED: compile strict isolated-module and isolated-declaration
        consumers for exact app/machine/recipe options and checkpoint-name accumulation; reject
        missing/extra fields, void input, context-free context, missing fixture closure,
        duplicate/widened names, and unknown literal keys.

        - Extend one common prefix three ways and run twice; pass when the prefix is unchanged and
        each run has fresh Runtime, fixture/provider state, Persistence, handles, and command
        replay. Construction runs nothing.

        - Planned checks: check:types, check:packed-consumers, and tests under
        packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/testing/test. These proposed contract checks remain
        pending.
        - For the inferred Story result, assert the exact two-service requirement union and
        covariance. Remove only Flow-owned Scope; complete fixtures do not turn it into never.

    V2  Fixture closure, recipe identity, dependency ownership, and failure cleanup
        - Establishes @O2. PLANNED: repeated recipe identity, distinct equal recipes, repeated
        provider slots, stable/transitive refs, provider override, duplicate fixtures, service
        acquisition failure, cancellation, and seeds.

        - Pass with exact requirement closure, one provider build per Runtime, providers before
        consumers, leases retained/reversed, and shared actors still Runtime-owned. Reject
        foreign/cyclic/incomplete recipes before actor or external work.

        - Prove seeds never satisfy services or authorize writes and kernels remain
        production-owned. Planned checks: production Story fixture/recipe tests plus
        check:types; tests remain pending.
        - For an inferred fixture result, assert the exact service requirement union and
        Scope removal under @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O2. Seeds cannot erase its carrier.

    V3  Production processing, explicit TestClock, maxTurns, and every exit lane
        - Establishes @O3. PLANNED: acknowledged FIFO sends, pending external calls/streams, ready
        reconciliation/context/timers, changed/equal setContext, maxTurns exhaustion, and
        clock-only movement. Advance time without process; pass only when no implicit work
        drains.

        - Verify @plan.Statecharts.V4 timer refresh fencing through this host without duplicating
        its owner matrix. Cancel before/during commands and each cleanup phase; inject
        independent cleanup failures.

        - Leave a provider permanently suspended while Story send waits behind a blocked context
        head. Cancel the run. Runtime terminal disposal retires that lane under
        @plan.ApplicationsAndActors.O5, settles the waiting acknowledgment once and finishes
        cleanup without upstream recovery. It fabricates no committed turn or successful end.

        - Pass with closed admission, no later command, all finalizers attempted once,
        deterministic ordering, preserved primary failure, full aggregated Cause, and no success
        after cleanup failure. Planned production Story/TestClock tests remain unimplemented.
    V4  Atomic exact evidence, capture races, and truthful failures
        - Establishes @O4. PLANNED: checkpoints for app recipe/ref and focused snapshot, unknown
        and inherited lookup, unrelated active actors, concurrent store/context publication,
        suspended debt, failure before end capture, and failure after end capture.

        - Pass with exact membership, one committed store cut, causally complete context or
        ConcurrentCapture, no live capture work, immutable earlier evidence, and no fabricated
        end. Cleanup failure retains captured end but rejects.

        - Compare direct/CLI evidence, diagnostics, Cause projection, and accepted/ drained
        prefix. Planned Story/barrier/artifact integration tests remain unimplemented.
    V5  Pure model oracle and ordinary live-path parity
        - Establishes @O5. PLANNED: independent small graph oracle for BFS/DFS order, zero-step
        paths, cycles, equal canonical keys, both limits, hidden candidates,
        noncanonical/defective keys, separate traversal event sets, and guard-relevant
        memory/seed changes.

        - Pass with exact order/results/truncation, no partial failures, no acquisition/mutation,
        and call-local events. Run final/prefix Stories through production and compare stateKey,
        snapshots, turns, pending work, operation facts/generations, context, and cleanup.

        - Declare only genuinely nondeterministic field exceptions. Async candidate events must be
        authored. React remains outside machine time. Planned model purity/oracle and
        live-parity tests remain pending.
        - Assert RequirementsOf on the inferred model equals its base machine Story's exact
        requirement union, with covariance and only Flow-owned Scope removed.

    V6  Branded gateway identity and inert discovery
        - Establishes @O6. PLANNED: valid App/machine Story records and all invalid app/ID/recipe
        combinations.

        - Pass when registration is synchronous, one supplied App identity is retained, type/brand
        requirements reject lookalikes, and no provider/Runtime/fixture/sink/executor is
        acquired. Pair with @plan.CommandLine.V1 installed-loader integration.

        - Planned gateway production tests and packed type consumers remain unimplemented.
questions
    Q1  Which readonly members and types does FlowStoryExecutionError expose?
        @plan.Verification.V4 and @O3 describe its evidence but leave its public member shape open.

    Q2  What does model result explored count, and when does each item count?
        @O5 and @V5 do not define the counted unit or its increment point.

    Q3  Which input type and units does advance(duration) accept?
        @O3 names the command without fixing its duration representation.

    Q4  What is the machine-state member name in stateKey's predicted snapshot?
        The older @plan.PublicSurface.O5 example uses value; a prior proposed example used state.
        Neither defines that exact snapshot member.
